  [OK] P3-KEY *** THE FRAMEWORK'S kappa = 1/2 IS FAVOURED OVER MILGROM 2020's 1/2pi BY THIS FRONT. *** Dchi2 = 63.9 vs 154.3, a difference of 90.4 (2.16 sigma on the conservative clustered counting). The gap IS resolvable and it resolves the framework's way -- but see P4: both sit LOW of the free best fit, which is the honest other edge
  [OK] P3-BOTHWAYS and the standing rule earns its keep: the ALTERNATIVE footing (1.13e-10) fits BETTER than the canonical one, Dchi2 7.0 vs 63.9. This front prefers a HIGHER a0 than 9.36e-11, so it is not a clean win for the canonical footing -- it is a win for kappa = 1/2 over 1/2pi and a pull toward the alternative footing at the same time

  CONTROL -- deep-MOND points only (g_bar/a0 < 0.3). My first version of this control REFUTED my
  own P1a: deep points constrain a0 fine. P1a' says why -- the GAS anchors it. So the mutation that
  isolates the mechanism is to let Upsilon scale the GAS TOO, restoring the exact degeneracy:
    a0/a0_fw   Dchi2 real   Dchi2 gas-scaled
        0.70         0.00              51.42
        1.00        27.23               3.81
        1.15       145.02               0.00
        1.45       500.57               3.52
  [OK] P3-CONTROL *** the mechanism is the GAS, verified by mutation. *** On the SAME deep-MOND points, letting Upsilon scale the gas as well -- which restores P1a's exact invariance -- collapses the a0 constraint from Dchi2 span 500.6 to 51.4, a 10x loss. Delete the Upsilon-free baryon component and the constraint dies, exactly as P1a' predicts. So the 1.24% is physics, not point-counting
  [OK] P3a the profile likelihood is bounded on BOTH sides (crossings at 1.095e-10 and 1.068e-10), so a0 is genuinely measured here, not merely bounded -- the per-galaxy Upsilon freedom did NOT flatten it
  [OK] P3b and it is FAR tighter than the per-point scatter: sigma(a0) = 1.24% against the 28% I used in the survey. Freeing Upsilon per galaxy costs 175 parameters and the shape lever of P1b still pins a0 -- so 28% was never the error on a0

